CONTEXT:
The Create Rule page (Next.js + Tailwind) has grown past 3,000 lines in one component. 
It combines: General Details fields (Entity/Field Group/Name/Description/Return Code, 
API-populated), a recursive nested AND/OR condition builder (groups can contain 
conditions or further nested groups, arbitrary depth), a flat Actions section, a live 
JSON preview panel, full recursive field-level validation, custom searchable-dropdown 
components used for every selection control, and maker-checker workflow buttons gating 
what's editable/submittable/approvable.

GOAL: split into a well-organized set of smaller files with ZERO observable behavior 
change — same validation messages, same generated JSON shape, same dropdown search/
positioning behavior, same responsive breakpoints, same API calls, same maker-checker 
button gating. This is a relocation of code, not a rewrite.

STEP 1 — AUDIT + SAFETY NET (do this first, don't move any code yet):
1. Read the current file fully. Map: state variables and their shapes, which functions 
   validate, which build the preview JSON, which fetch data, which render which visual 
   section, where the searchable-dropdown logic lives, how maker-checker permission 
   flags gate the button bar.
2. Confirm the current state-management approach (multiple useState, one useReducer, 
   Formik/React Hook Form, something else) — the plan below assumes it can be relocated 
   as-is, not redesigned.
3. Before moving anything, write characterization tests (Playwright, reusing the setup 
   from the testing work already planned) against the CURRENT page:
   - Submit with everything empty → every "is required" message from the screenshots 
     appears in the right place
   - A simple single-condition rule produces the exact JSON shape currently shown in 
     the preview panel (down to field names like isActionGroup, logical_operator, not)
   - Adding a nested OR group renders correctly and nests correctly in the output JSON
   - The searchable Select Field dropdown filters correctly on typed input
   These tests must pass before AND after every extraction step below — they're the 
   actual proof that nothing broke, not a visual check.

STEP 2 — TARGET STRUCTURE (extract by responsibility):
  /features/rule-builder/
    CreateRulePage.tsx            — thin orchestrator only: holds/provides state, loads 
                                     existing record on edit (via the WorkflowRecordLoader 
                                     pattern already planned), composes sections below
    components/
      GeneralDetailsSection.tsx
      InputConditionsSection.tsx  — renders the top-level ConditionGroup
      ConditionGroup.tsx          — RECURSIVE: a group renders a list of rows, each 
                                     either a leaf ConditionRow or another ConditionGroup
      ConditionRow.tsx
      ActionsSection.tsx
      ActionRow.tsx
      RulePreviewPanel.tsx
      FormActionBar.tsx           — Close/Reset/Create/Submit/Approve/Reject, reads 
                                     permissions from the workflow loader already 
                                     designed, doesn't recompute its own gating logic
    hooks/
      useEntities.ts / useFieldGroups.ts / useReturnCodes.ts / 
      useFieldsForEntity.ts / useOperators.ts   — one hook per data source, isolates 
                                     fetch + loading + error state
      useRuleForm.ts               — wraps whatever state mechanism Step 1 finds, 
                                     exposed via context so sections don't need a huge 
                                     prop list
    lib/
      validateRule.ts              — pure function, recursively walks the tree, returns 
                                     an errors map; no JSX, no React, unit-testable
      buildRulePayload.ts          — pure function, form state → the exact JSON shape 
                                     shown in the preview
  /components/shared/
    SearchableSelect.tsx           — ONE generic, prop-driven component (options, 
                                     onSelect, placeholder) used for every dropdown 
                                     currently duplicated across Entity/Field Group/
                                     Return Code/Field/Operations/Operator

STEP 3 — MIGRATION ORDER (risk-ordered, lowest first):
1. SearchableSelect first — most self-contained, proves the extraction pattern is safe.
2. ActionsSection next — flat, non-recursive, simpler than the condition tree.
3. GeneralDetailsSection.
4. validateRule + buildRulePayload — lift the existing logic verbatim, don't reimplement 
   from a fresh read of what it "should" do; a rewrite risks silently changing a 
   validation message or a JSON field name.
5. ConditionGroup LAST, once the pattern is proven — it's recursive, so a subtle bug 
   (lost key prop, off-by-one in depth) is much harder to spot visually than in the 
   flatter sections. Lean hardest on the nested-OR-group characterization test here.

For each step: move the code as-is, wire it back in, run the full characterization 
suite, confirm parity, commit — then move to the next. Don't batch multiple extractions 
into one commit; if something regresses, you want to know which extraction did it.

DELIVERABLES:
1. Characterization test suite, committed before any file is touched
2. Target folder structure above
3. One commit per extraction, in the order in Step 3, each passing the full suite
4. Final CreateRulePage doing orchestration only — no file should be doing more than 
   one of: fetching, validating, building payload, or rendering a section
5. Written note on anything found during the audit that looks ambiguous or possibly 
   already a bug (e.g. the id-generation scheme for condition nodes) — flag it, don't 
   silently "fix" it mid-refactor

CONSTRAINTS:
- Zero visible behavior change, full stop
- No new dependencies — no new form library, no new state library — as part of this 
  pass, even if one would be cleaner; that's a separate decision with its own risk
- Move code, never rewrite it, during extraction
- ConditionGroup is the last extraction, not the first